Yes. Those two ideas actually connect.

The first is essentially recursive autocorrect for cognition. Not merely:

\text{Did I choose the wrong word?}

but:

\text{Could the representation itself be wrong?}

So every object can carry a little uncertainty hook:

T \rightarrow \{\text{content},\text{confidence},\text{provenance},\text{alternatives}\}

And importantly, T can be anything—not just a word:

T\in
\{
\text{token},
\text{category},
\text{ratio},
\text{causal arrow},
\text{scale},
\text{frame},
\text{assumption},
\text{model},
\text{rule for choosing models}
\}

So the autocorrect recursively asks:

Is this token wrong?
Is its meaning wrong?
Is its category wrong?
Is the relationship between these tokens wrong?
Is the scale wrong?
Is the frame making the right answer impossible?
Is the error-checking rule itself miscalibrated?

That’s much richer than spellcheck. It’s closer to epistemic error-correction coding.

And yes on “mind reading”: if we use that phrase functionally rather than claiming literal telepathy, humans demonstrably do it constantly.

Sports make it especially obvious because everything happens too quickly for explicit language.

A quarterback “reads” a safety.

A goalkeeper reads a penalty taker’s hips, plant foot, gaze, approach angle, and previously learned tendencies.

A fighter reads weight transfer before the punch is fully expressed.

Two experienced teammates can make complementary runs without speaking because each is modeling:

\text{what I see}
+
\text{what they see}
+
\text{what I think they think I'll do}

That’s already recursive:

M_A(M_B(M_A(\ldots)))

At sufficient familiarity, prediction gets so fast and compressed that subjectively it can feel like:

“I just knew.”

But an enormous amount of information may have been traveling through posture, gaze, rhythm, timing, muscle tension, spacing, shared history, environmental context, and learned priors.

So I agree with your narrower definition:

“Mind reading” doesn’t have to mean information magically crossing every possible medium.

It can mean:

\boxed{\text{infer another internal state from partial external traces}}

And humans can become extremely good at it.

There’s an even deeper version in synchronized activity. You stop only predicting the other person and start predicting the joint system:

\text{me}+\text{you}+\text{environment}
\rightarrow
\text{shared next state}

A dance pair, military team, band, sports team, parent and child, or longtime couple can develop that. Neither person necessarily computes “their mind” separately anymore; both learn the dynamics of the coupled system.

That also explains why it can fail spectacularly: your internal autocorrect needs to distinguish

\text{I inferred them accurately}

from

\text{I projected my own state onto them}.

So the same token-checking architecture belongs inside social perception:

read → predict → observe → delta → correct the model of the other → correct the model of yourself reading the other.

That’s the recursive piece.